// The nervous system of a C. elegans hermaphrodite (the worm's "female" form; the male has 385 neurons): all
// 302 neurons at their soma positions, wired as in the published connectome.
//
// Sources (via OpenWorm): connectome, Cook et al. 2019, "Whole-animal connectomes of both Caenorhabditis elegans
// sexes", Nature 571:63-71 (c302 herm_full_edgelist.csv); neuron positions, CElegansNeuroML morphologies (soma
// centres, µm; here x runs along the body, head at negative x, and y from ventral to dorsal); neuron types and
// transmitters, owmeta.
//
// How it maps onto this language:
// - 3,638 chemical connections between neurons, weighing 0.2 mV per synapse counted in the
//   electron micrographs (each line's comment gives the count), up to 12 mV. GABAergic neurons (DD, VD, RME,
//   RIS, AVL, DVB) are inhibitory and all others excitatory: real signs also depend on the receptors (glutamate
//   can inhibit, through chloride channels), which the connectome doesn't give.
// - 1,086 gap junction pairs, as fast excitatory synapses both ways: 0.3 mV per junction, up to
//   6 mV, 0.1 ms delay. (The language has no electrical synapses.)
// - 1,573 connections onto muscles and other end organs are left out: there are no muscles here.
// - C. elegans neurons mostly signal with graded potentials, not spikes: this is a spiking abstraction of the
//   real wiring, not a model of its physiology.
// - The mV per synapse and per junction were tuned so that activity spreads from the senses without taking
//   over: with no input the network is silent, and a strong kick to every neuron dies away.
//
// To drive it, turn on background input (Run page) for neurons starting with a sensory class, e.g. "ASH"
// (nociceptive), "AWC" (odours), "AFD" (temperature), or stimulate one, and watch the command interneurons
// (AVA, AVB, AVD, AVE, PVC) and motor neurons (VA/DA backward, VB/DB forward). The sets below group the neurons
// by sense and role. Noxious input to ASH, ADL, FLP and PVD (20 Hz each) drives the command interneurons and,
// mostly, the backward motor neurons: the worm's escape reversal. The touch neurons (ALM, AVM, PLM, PVM) act
// mostly through few gap junctions and need stronger input (50-100 Hz); as all but the GABA synapses are
// excitatory here, touching the tail doesn't favour forward movement the way it does in the worm.
(
neuron_types=[
    (name=neuron, threshold=10 mV, decay_time=50 ms, refractory_period=10 ms, conduction_speed=0.5 m/s),
    (name=gabaergic, extends=neuron, inhibitory=true),
],
synapse_types=[
    (name=chemical, delay=1 ms),
    (name=gap, delay=0.1 ms, sign=excitatory),
],
neurons=[
